    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 10,
      "title": "User Threads",
      "images": 1,
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "User Threads",
          "kind": "heading",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Thread management done by user-level threads library",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "All of the thread management is done by the application and the kernel is not aware of the existence of threads.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Three primary thread libraries:",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "POSIX Pthreads",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Win32 threads",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Java threads",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Advantages of user level threads:",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "There are a number of advantages to the use of ULTs instead of KLTs",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Thread switching does not require kernel mode privileges",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Scheduling can be application specific",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "ULTs can run on any OS.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Disadvantages of user level threads:",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "When a ULT executes a blocking system call, not only is that thread blocked, but also all of the threads within the process are blocked.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "A Multithreaded application cannot take advantage of multiprocessing. A kernel assigns one process to only one processor at a time.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 11,
      "title": "Kernel Threads",
      "images": 1,
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Kernel Threads",
          "kind": "heading",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Supported by the Kernel",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "There is no thread management code in the",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "application level, simply an application programming",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "interface (API) to the kernel thread facility.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "The kernel maintains context information",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "for the process as a whole and for individual threads",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "within the process.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Examples",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Windows XP/2000",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Solaris",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Linux",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Tru64 UNIX",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Mac OS X",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Advantages of Kernel level threads:",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "The kernel can simultaneously schedule multiple threads from the same process on multiple processors.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "If one thread in a process is blocked, the kernel can schedule another thread of the same process.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Disadvantages of kernel level threads:",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "The transfer of control from one thread to another within the same process requires a mode switch to the kernel.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        }
      ]
    },
