    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 27,
      "title": "Semantics of fork() and exec()",
      "images": 0,
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Semantics of fork() and exec()",
          "kind": "heading",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Does fork() duplicate only the calling thread or all threads?",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "exec() - replace the running process including all threads",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 28,
      "title": "Thread Cancellation",
      "images": 0,
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Thread Cancellation",
          "kind": "heading",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Terminating a thread before it has finished",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Two general approaches:",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Asynchronous cancellation terminates the target thread immediately.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Deferred cancellation allows the target thread to periodically check if it should be cancelled.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 29,
      "title": "Signal Handling",
      "images": 0,
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Signal Handling",
          "kind": "heading",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Signals are used in UNIX systems to notify a process that a particular event has occurred.",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "A signal handler is used to process signals",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Signal is generated by particular event",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Signal is delivered to a process",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Signal is handled",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Options:",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Deliver the signal to the thread to which the signal applies",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Deliver the signal to every thread in the process",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Deliver the signal to certain threads in the process",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Assign a specific thread to receive all signals for the process",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 30,
      "title": "Thread Pools",
      "images": 0,
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Thread Pools",
          "kind": "heading",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Create a number of threads in a pool where they await work",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Advantages:",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Usually slightly faster to service a request with an existing thread than create a new thread",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Allows the number of threads in the application(s) to be bound to the size of the pool",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 31,
      "title": "Thread Specific Data",
      "images": 0,
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Thread Specific Data",
          "kind": "heading",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Allows each thread to have its own copy of data",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Useful when you do not have control over the thread creation process (i.e., when using a thread pool)",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 32,
      "title": "Scheduler Activations",
      "images": 0,
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Scheduler Activations",
          "kind": "heading",
          "level": 1,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Both M:M and Two-level models require communication to maintain the appropriate number of kernel threads allocated to the application",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "Scheduler activations provide upcalls - a communication mechanism from the kernel to the thread library",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        },
        {
          "text": "This communication allows an application to maintain the correct number kernel threads",
          "kind": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "para_break": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "slide",
      "number": 33,
      "title": "Lightweight Processes",
      "images": 1,
